<GuideLayout slug="hurricane-tree-preparation">
  <p>
    Atlantic hurricane season runs from June 1 to November 30. In South Florida, trees cause a large share of storm
    damage: broken limbs through roofs, uprooted trees across driveways, and branches pulling down power lines. Most of
    that damage starts with problems that were visible long before the storm. Here is what to check, and when.
  </p>

  <h2>Before the season: check every tree near the house</h2>
  <p>Walk around your property in spring and look for these warning signs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dead or hanging branches</strong>, especially over the roof, driveway or pool.</li>
    <li><strong>Cracks or splits</strong> in the trunk or where large limbs join the trunk.</li>
    <li><strong>A new or worsening lean</strong>, or soil lifting on one side of the tree.</li>
    <li><strong>Fungus, mushrooms or soft, hollow wood</strong> at the base, signs of decay inside.</li>
    <li><strong>Branches touching or overhanging power lines</strong>. Never trim these yourself; call your utility.</li>
    <li><strong>Trees that are known to fail in wind</strong>, such as Australian pine.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you find any of these, have the tree looked at. A <a href="/certified-arborist/">certified arborist</a> can tell you
    whether pruning is enough or whether the tree should come down, and document it if a permit exemption applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Prune the right way, early</h2>
  <p>
    Good structural pruning lets wind pass through the canopy and removes weak limbs before they fail. It works best
    well before a storm is forecast, not the week before landfall, when crews are booked and debris pickup may not happen
    in time.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Thin the canopy rather than cutting the top off. "Hatracking" weakens trees and makes them more dangerous.</li>
    <li>Remove dead wood and branches rubbing against the roof.</li>
    <li>For palms, remove dead fronds and coconuts, but don't over-prune; green fronds protect the palm's bud.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When a storm is coming</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Don't start cutting trees once a storm is announced. Piles of fresh debris can turn into projectiles.</li>
    <li>Follow your city's guidance on yard waste. Many cities stop debris pickup before a storm.</li>
    <li>Move cars away from large trees if you can.</li>
    <li>Take photos of your trees and property. They help with insurance claims afterwards.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>After the storm: stay safe first</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stay away from any tree or branch touching power lines.</strong> Assume the line is live and report it to your utility.</li>
    <li>Don't climb into damaged trees or cut limbs under tension. Broken wood can spring or roll without warning.</li>
    <li>Photograph the damage before anything is moved, for your insurance claim.</li>
    <li>Watch out for crews going door to door. Ask for proof of insurance before anyone starts work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For a tree on your house, car or driveway, send us the
    <a href="/contact-us/">contact form</a> and choose emergency tree removal. See <a href="/emergency-tree-removal/">emergency tree removal</a>
    for what happens when we arrive.
  </p>

  <h2>A simple yearly routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Spring:</strong> walk the property and note any warning signs.</li>
    <li><strong>Before June:</strong> have risky trees assessed, pruned or removed.</li>
    <li><strong>During the season:</strong> keep an eye on new damage after every strong storm.</li>
    <li><strong>After the season:</strong> clean up and plan any removals for the drier months.</li>
  </ol>
</GuideLayout>
